# feat: Web UI Port — Browser Build + v1 Frontend Look on the XPBD Rewrite

## Summary

Bring the **original v1 web UI's look** to the rewrite by standing up the rewrite's (currently
deferred) **wasm + WebGPU browser build** and porting the original web frontend to drive the new
XPBD solver. The rewrite is native-only today (`src/ui/` is winit + wgpu); the original is a
working browser app (`crates/sim-wasm/www-3d/` on the `main` branch) — and crucially it is built on
**the same wgpu 29.0.1**, so the WebGPU/canvas plumbing ports almost verbatim.

The work is a thin shell around code that already exists, not a rewrite of the renderer or solver:
- The rewrite's `ui::Renderer` is **surface-agnostic** (`render(target_view, particles, camera)` —
  no winit coupling) and already draws sphere-impostor particles. It is reused as-is; only
  canvas-surface creation is new.
- The browser owns the page, the controls, and the `requestAnimationFrame` loop (as v1 did); wasm
  is the **simulate + render core**. `winit` stays a native-only dependency.
- A small `#[wasm_bindgen]` handle exposes the rewrite's `Simulator`/`Renderer`/`OrbitCamera` to JS,
  mirroring v1's `WasmSim3D` method set but slimmed to the controls and metrics the rewrite has.
- `www/index.html` + `styles.css` are ported near-verbatim for the **core visible UI**; `main.js`
  is adapted to the new API.

**Scope (per the confirmed "core visible UI first" decision):** sidebar (pause/reset, scene
buttons, water-velocity slider, spout pad + height, Particles/FPS), the viewer canvas, the CSS
view-cube, the cross-section overlay frame, and a **brew scorecard** wired to the rewrite's real
metrics (extraction yield, TDS, evenness, drawdown time). The large MPM-specific debug-stats panel
and the timeseries drawer are **deferred** — their fields mostly have no XPBD analogue.

---

## Problem Frame

`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` §2 names a browser build (wasm + WebGPU) as the eventual reach goal but
explicitly deferred it "until the sim is in good shape." The XPBD primary solver is now
substantially complete, and the next block in the build order (§6) is `vis/scorecard`. The user
wants the rewrite to **look like the original web app**, whose look *is* an HTML/CSS web UI — so
matching it means bringing the deferred web build online and porting the frontend.

The original (`crates/sim-wasm/` on `main`, read via `git show main:<path>`) is the blueprint: a
`#[wasm_bindgen]` core (`lib.rs`/`renderer.rs`) that JS drives via `requestAnimationFrame`, a
canvas-backed `wgpu::Surface`, and a hand-written `www-3d/` frontend (no bundler; built with
`wasm-pack --target web`). Because it is the **same wgpu 29**, the surface/init code is a direct
template rather than a research project.

The mismatch to bridge: the original is **MPM** (its debug stats — projection residual, `div u`,
pressure pairs — don't exist in XPBD) and its scenes differ from the rewrite's `Scene`
constructors. The core UI ports directly; the MPM-specific surfaces remap or defer.

---

## Requirements

**Build & portability**
- R1. The crate builds for **both** native (`cargo build`, unchanged) and `wasm32-unknown-unknown`
  (`wasm-pack build --target web`), sharing solver/renderer code via `cfg(target_arch)` gating.
  `winit`/`pollster` become native-only deps; wasm deps are web-only.
- R2. The browser build targets **WebGPU** (not WebGL2), runs against `wgpu::Limits::default()`
  (WebGPU baseline, ≤ 8 storage buffers/stage), and **never raises the device storage-buffer
  limit** (v1's fatal web mistake — see `project_v1_lessons`).
- R3. Native behavior is byte-unchanged: the async-init refactor and the `CurrentSurfaceTexture`
  reshape leave the native render/test paths working (full `cargo test` suite green except the
  documented pre-existing reds).

**Browser core (wasm)**
- R4. A WebGPU surface is created from an HTML `<canvas>` and the adapter/device are requested
  **asynchronously** (no `pollster::block_on` on wasm; `wasm-bindgen-futures`).
- R5. A `#[wasm_bindgen]` handle exposes: async construction from a canvas, `step_frame(dt)`,
  `render()`, `reset()`, `resize`/`resizeWithCssSize`, camera orbit/zoom/pan + yaw/pitch getters,
  scene loaders, the control setters below, and metric/particle-count getters — driving the solver
  **only** through `EmissionInput`/`Scene` and reading back **only** canonical `State`/`Metrics`
  (one-way data flow).

**Controls & scenes (look the same)**
- R6. The **water-velocity (m/s) slider** and the **2D spout-position pad + height** drive the
  pour: water velocity → `EmissionInput.flow_rate` via the nozzle effective area + the unit scale
  (mirroring v1's exit-speed→flow); spout x/z/height → `EmissionInput.kettle_pos`.
- R7. Pause/Reset and the scene buttons work: **Center Pour → `Scene::v60_pour()`** (continuous
  pour via a `PourScript`), **Water Only → `Scene::dam_break()`**.
- R8. The **brew scorecard** displays the rewrite's real metrics — extraction yield, TDS, evenness,
  drawdown time — plus Particles and a JS-side FPS counter.

**Look**
- R9. The ported `index.html` + `styles.css` reproduce the original's core layout and theme
  (sidebar + viewer grid, panel styling, CSS view-cube driven by camera yaw/pitch, cross-section
  overlay frame) for the in-scope surfaces.

---

## Key Technical Decisions

- KTD-1 — **Single crate, dual target via `crate-type = ["cdylib", "rlib"]` + cfg-gating.** No new
  crate or workspace member — mirror v1's `["cdylib", "rlib"]` so the same crate produces the
  native lib/tests (`rlib`) and the browser `.wasm` (`cdylib`). `winit`/`pollster` move under
  `[target.'cfg(not(target_arch = "wasm32"))'.dependencies]`; wasm deps (`wasm-bindgen`,
  `wasm-bindgen-futures`, `web-sys`, `js-sys`, `console_error_panic_hook`, `console_log`/`log`,
  `getrandom` with `wasm_js`) under the wasm-cfg target. The wasm-bindgen handle lives in `lib.rs`
  behind `#[cfg(target_arch = "wasm32")]`.
- KTD-2 — **Bypass winit on the web; JS owns the rAF loop.** winit-on-web only buys cross-platform
  input/window plumbing the JS frontend already owns. The browser holds the canvas, the rAF loop,
  resize, and DOM events, and calls wasm exports — exactly v1's shape. This keeps winit a
  native-only dep and the wasm boundary minimal. (Researched: winit 0.30 *does* support web via
  `spawn_app`, but it's overhead here.)
- KTD-3 — **Reuse `ui::Renderer` unchanged; add only a canvas surface.** `Renderer::render` already
  takes a `TextureView` target + `ParticleBuffers` + `OrbitCamera` (surface-agnostic, no winit).
  The only new GPU code is a `GpuContext::new_web(canvas)` that builds the surface from
  `wgpu::SurfaceTarget::Canvas` and `.await`s adapter/device — the v1 `Renderer::new` async block is
  the direct template (same wgpu 29). Particle data stays GPU-side and never crosses to JS (as in
  v1); JS reads only counts/metrics.
- KTD-4 — **wgpu-29 web reshapes (researched, version-specific).** `web_sys_unstable_apis` is
  **not** needed (wgpu 29 vendors its WebGPU bindings). The web instance uses
  `Instance::new(InstanceDescriptor { backends: Backends::BROWSER_WEBGPU, ..new_without_display_handle() })`
  to force WebGPU. `get_current_texture()` returns a `CurrentSurfaceTexture` **enum** in v29 — the
  acquire site becomes a `match` (reconfigure on `Outdated`/`Suboptimal`, skip on
  `Timeout`/`Occluded`), which also updates the native path. Surface config comes from
  `surface.get_capabilities(&adapter)` (`Fifo` present mode, caps format; `Depth32Float` is
  WebGPU-baseline — safe). A `.cargo/config.toml` sets `--cfg getrandom_backend="wasm_js"` for the
  transitive `getrandom 0.3`.
- KTD-5 — **Timestamp profiling is native-only on web.** WebGPU baseline lacks `TIMESTAMP_QUERY`.
  It already self-disables via `GpuContext.timestamps_supported`, but the QuerySet creation +
  `get_timestamp_period` readback in the XPBD solver are `#[cfg(not(target_arch = "wasm32"))]`-gated
  so no timestamp resources are constructed on web.
- KTD-6 — **Preserve the water-velocity (m/s) control by converting to volumetric flow.** The
  rewrite drives `EmissionInput.flow_rate` (sim-units³/s), not exit speed — but v1 itself mapped a
  m/s slider to flow via the nozzle. Convert `v_m/s → flow_rate` through the emitter's effective
  area `A_eff = π·nozzle_radius²·discharge_coeff` and the unit scale (`SIM_UNITS_PER_METER`,
  `ML_PER_SIM_UNIT3` from `KEEP.md`), so the slider keeps its label and feel. (Alternative —
  relabel to "Flow (mL/s)" — is simpler but changes the look; deferred unless the conversion proves
  unstable.)
- KTD-7 — **CSS view-cube, not the GPU gizmo, on web.** The rewrite's `Renderer` draws a GPU
  orientation cube (`gizmo.wgsl`); v1 used a CSS `.view-cube` driven by `cameraYaw/cameraPitch`. To
  match the original's look, expose yaw/pitch getters and drive the CSS cube; **skip the GPU gizmo
  pass on web** so they don't double up. (`OrbitCamera` exposes `yaw`/`pitch` already.)
- KTD-8 — **Build with `wasm-pack --target web` into `www/pkg/`; no bundler.** Matches v1: a plain
  ES-module `main.js` imports `./pkg/coffee_sim.js`; serve `www/` statically. A `navigator.gpu`
  preflight shows a fallback message instead of initializing when WebGPU is absent.

