## Alternatives Considered

- **Native UI (egui/custom) instead of the web port.** Faster to a usable dev UI, but it cannot
  match the original's HTML/CSS look (the whole point) and diverges from the architecture's web
  goal. Rejected for this objective.
- **winit-on-web** (reuse the native event loop via `spawn_app`). Viable in winit 0.30, but adds an
  abstraction between JS and the render core when JS already owns the page; more code for no benefit
  here (KTD-2). Rejected; winit stays native-only.
- **Port v1's `renderer.rs`** instead of reusing the rewrite's. The rewrite's `ui::Renderer` is
  already surface-agnostic, sphere-impostor, and within the 8-buffer budget; porting v1's MPM-coupled
  renderer would duplicate it and re-introduce v1's render-buffer layout. Reuse (KTD-3) is less code
  and stays on the current data path.
- **Relabel the velocity slider to "Flow (mL/s)."** Simpler wiring, but changes the visible control
  vs the original. Kept the m/s label via the area+unit conversion (KTD-6); the relabel is the
  fallback if the conversion proves unstable.
- **Separate `coffee-sim-web` crate.** Cleaner dep isolation, but a second crate + workspace member
  is more moving parts than cfg-gating a single crate; v1 used a separate crate only because it
  wrapped a separate `sim-core`. Single-crate dual-target (KTD-1) fits the rewrite's one-crate
  architecture.

---

## Scope Boundaries

**In scope:** the dual-target build, the canvas surface + async init, the wgpu-29 render-loop
reshape, the `CoffeeSimApp` handle, and the **core visible frontend** (sidebar controls, viewer,
CSS view-cube, cross-section overlay frame, brew scorecard wired to real XPBD metrics), built with
`wasm-pack --target web`.

### Deferred to Follow-Up Work
- **MPM debug-stats panel** (`#debug-stats`, ~40 rows: projection residual, `div u`, pressure pairs,
  clamp fires, etc.). Most have no XPBD analogue; the XPBD diagnostics that do (yield/TDS/evenness/
  drawdown/iteration-residual/overflow) land in the scorecard now. A later pass can add an
  XPBD-specific debug panel.
- **Timeseries charts drawer** (`#timeseries-drawer`) — needs a JS charting layer + a metrics-sampling
  cadence; deferred.
- **Cross-section render pass.** The CSS overlay frame ships now; the actual GPU cross-section
  viewport (a second render pipeline with a clip plane, which v1 had and the rewrite's `Renderer`
  lacks) is a renderer feature deferred to a follow-up. This is the one place "look the same" is
  partial at first (frame present, image not).
- **Debug-scene grid** (13 MPM debug scenes) — no rewrite analogue; deferred.
- **Concentration→color and screen-space fluid** rendering (`vis.md` phase 3) — the web port reuses
  the current sphere-impostor look; richer fluid rendering is separate `vis` work.

### Outside this phase
- **Device-scaling** (adaptive particle/grid/iteration budgets from device limits) — explicitly
  deferred in `ARCHITECTURE.md` §2; not needed for a first browser build.
- **Browser performance profiling** — deferred per `PERF_NOTES.md`; native timings remain the proxy
  and timestamp queries are native-only (KTD-5).
- **Deployment/hosting** (CI wasm build, static hosting) — local `wasm-pack` + static server only.

---

## Risks & Dependencies

- **8-storage-buffer ceiling (v1's fatal web mistake).** *Mitigation:* the rewrite already pins
  `wgpu::Limits::default()` on native, so any over-budget pass fails on the dev machine, not just
  the browser; never raise the device limit to fix a binding error (R2). Already satisfied (the
  render pipeline binds 3 storage buffers).
- **wgpu-29 `get_current_texture` / surface-status churn.** The acquire reshape touches the native
  path too. *Mitigation:* U3 is a native-safe refactor gated by `tests/render_smoke.rs` staying
  green before the web path is added.
- **wasm toolchain friction** (getrandom `wasm_js` cfg, wasm-bindgen↔CLI version match). *Mitigation:*
  `.cargo/config.toml` rustflag (KTD-4); use `wasm-pack` (pins the matching CLI) rather than raw
  `wasm-bindgen` (KTD-8). U1 gates the wasm link before any frontend work.
- **No headless WebGPU in CI.** Web behavior can't be auto-tested. *Mitigation:* push logic into
  native-testable Rust (velocity→flow, spout mapping, scene selection — U4 tests); treat the
  frontend as manual browser verification and say so (U5); keep the native suite green (R3).
- **Velocity→flow conversion correctness/stability** (units across m/s → sim → mL → flow). *Mitigation:*
  unit-test the conversion natively against `KEEP.md` constants; the relabel-to-flow fallback exists
  (KTD-6).
- **Browser WebGPU availability.** *Mitigation:* `navigator.gpu` preflight + graceful fallback
  message (KTD-8); WebGPU is Baseline across major browsers as of Jan 2026, but Firefox-on-Linux and
  older OSes may lack it.
- **In-flight working tree.** The repo currently has the Phase 6 fines/channeling work committed and
  some untracked scratch; this port touches different files (build config, `gpu.rs`, `ui`, new
  `www/`), so conflict risk is low — but coordinate the `Cargo.toml`/`gpu.rs` edits with any other
  active branch.

**Verification gates (AGENTS.md):** `cargo fmt --check`; `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`
(native); `cargo test` (native, no new failures beyond the documented pre-existing reds);
`cargo build --target wasm32-unknown-unknown` + `wasm-pack build --target web` succeed; manual
WebGPU-browser smoke for the frontend. Stay within `wgpu::Limits::default()` / 8 storage buffers.

---

## Sources & Research

- **Original v1 frontend (the blueprint), on `main`:** `crates/sim-wasm/Cargo.toml` (wasm dep set +
  `crate-type`, **wgpu 29.0.1 — same as the rewrite**), `crates/sim-wasm/src/lib.rs` (the
  `WasmSim3D` `#[wasm_bindgen]` API to mirror), `crates/sim-wasm/src/renderer.rs` (canvas→surface
  async template), `crates/sim-wasm/www-3d/{index.html,styles.css,main.js}` (the look + rAF loop +
  control wiring to port). Read via `git show main:<path>`.
- **Rewrite reusable surfaces:** `src/ui/render.rs` (surface-agnostic `render(target_view,…)`,
  `Depth32Float`), `src/ui/camera.rs` (`OrbitCamera` with `yaw`/`pitch`), `src/engine/simulator.rs`
  + `src/engine/scene.rs` (`Simulator`, `Scene::v60_pour`/`dam_break`), `src/engine/state.rs`
  (`Metrics`: yield/tds/evenness/drawdown_time), `src/emission/{mod.rs,pour.rs}` (`EmissionInput`,
  `PourScript`, mL↔sim scale), `src/utils/gpu.rs` (the 3 `pollster::block_on` async seams),
  `src/utils/config.rs` (`nozzle_radius`/`discharge_coeff` → `A_eff`), `examples/water_app.rs` +
  `examples/pour.rs` (the native shell + `EmissionInput` driver to mirror).
- **External research (load-bearing — shaped KTD-1/2/4/5/8 and the render reshape):** wgpu 29
  changelog (`CurrentSurfaceTexture` enum; `InstanceDescriptor` ctor change; `web_sys_unstable_apis`
  no longer required), wgpu `SurfaceTarget::Canvas` + `Backends::BROWSER_WEBGPU`, winit 0.30 web
  (`spawn_app`, but bypassed here), `getrandom 0.3` `wasm_js` backend cfg, `wasm-pack --target web`,
  WebGPU Baseline across major browsers (Jan 2026), `Depth32Float` WebGPU-baseline, timestamp-query
  unavailability on web.
- **Learnings:** `project_v1_lessons` (8-buffer ceiling — never raise the device limit),
  `src/utils/gpu.rs` doc comments (the documented async-init porting seam), `AGENTS.md` (one-way
  data flow; WASM/WebGPU-portability is a stated goal; `Limits::default()`), `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`
  §2 (web build deferred but planned), `docs/plans/vis.md` (resolves its "shared web render
  pipeline" open question toward shared), `KEEP.md` (`ML_PER_SIM_UNIT3`, `SIM_UNITS_PER_METER`).

